{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Now I understand the test script. It's a Python script that checks:\n1. go build works\n2. go test ./internal/builder/... ./internal/prompt/... -v works\n3. templates unchanged\n4. rick plan --dry-run output contains path fragments and no unreplaced {{...}}\n5. PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\", nil) returns error with \"requirement cannot be empty\"\n6. Structural checks for internal/builder package: xxxxbuilder.go with RuntimeBuilder interface, Method/AgentDef types, PIBuilder type + methods, BuildPlan signature.\n\nThe test script is strict. Let me note the structural checks:\n- xxxxbuilder.go must contain exact text:\n  - `type RuntimeBuilder interface`\n  - `Name() string`\n  - `BuildAgents(method []Method) ([]AgentDef, error)`\n  - `BuildPrompt(cmd string, params map[string]string) (string, error)`\n- builder_src_txt must contain `type Method` and `type AgentDef` (via regex `\\btype Method\\b` and `\\btype AgentDef\\b`)\n- builder_src_txt must contain `type PIBuilder`\n- For each method: regex `\\)\\s*BuildPlan\\s*\\(` etc. This means the method must be a receiver method (with `)` before method name). Actually `\\)\\s*BuildPlan\\s*\\(` matches e.g. `func (p *PIBuilder) BuildPlan(...)`. Note: `)` followed by `BuildPlan(`.\n- BuildPlan signature: `BuildPlan(requirement string` present, and `(method string, instance string, err error)` present.\n\nWait, the regex for method is `\\)\\s*` + meth + `\\s*\\(`. For `func (p *PIBuilder) BuildPlan(requirement string, params map[string]string) (method string, instance string, err error) {` — this matches `) BuildPlan(`. Good.\n\nBut note: it checks the method string appears with a `)` before it. This means it must be a method (receiver). Good.\n\nNow, there's a subtlety: `BuildPlan(requirement string` — the test checks for this exact substring in builder_src_txt. My signature should be `BuildPlan(requirement string, params map[string]string)`. Good.\n\nAnd `(method string, instance string, err error)` must appear. So I must write return signature exactly like that.\n\nNow, the big question: what should the pibuilder methods actually DO and return?\n\nThe test only checks:\n- BuildPlan(\"\", nil) returns error containing \"requirement cannot be empty\".\n- The plan --dry-run output (via cmd/plan.go) contains path fragments `plan/task|doing/debug|/jobs/|/domain` and no `{{...}}`.\n\nSo I need to:\n1. Create `internal/builder` package with pibuilder.go and xxxxbuilder.go.\n2. Wire cmd layer to use builder package (at least for plan --dry-run, and ideally all commands).\n3. Ensure plan --dry-run output contains real path fragments and no unreplaced vars.\n\nLet me think about what \"inject paths instead of content\" means concretely for each command.\n\nFor **plan**:\n- Current `GeneratePlanPrompt` injects `loops_context` (content), `domain_dir` (path), `user_requirement` (content), `job_plan_dir` (path), `rick_bin_path` (path), `job_id`, skill paths.\n- The \"content → path\" change primarily affects `task_info_section` and `debug_context` in the doing template, and OKR/SPEC. Plan doesn't have task_info_section/debug_context.\n\nWait, but the boundary test checks `rick plan --dry-run | grep -cE 'plan/task|doing/debug|/jobs/|/domain'`. The plan template already injects `{{domain_dir}}` as a real path and `{{job_plan_dir}}` as a real path. But `--dry-run` uses `jobPlanDir = <rickDir>/jobs/job_N/plan`, so `/jobs/` and `/domain` would already appear in the current output. Actually wait — currently the plan dry-run calls `prompt.GeneratePlanPrompt` which sets `domain_dir` to `<rickDir>/domain` (real path, contains `/domain`), and `job_plan_dir` to `<rickDir>/jobs/job_N/plan` (real path, contains `/jobs/`). So the current output ALREADY matches `/jobs/` and `/domain`.\n\nBut the test ALSO checks for `plan/task` or `doing/debug`. The plan template does NOT contain \"plan/task\" or \"doing/debug\" currently. Hmm. So the boundary test wants `plan/task` or `doing/debug` to appear in plan --dry-run output? Wait, the regex is `plan/task|doing/debug|/jobs/|/domain`, and \"expect ≥1 line matching\". Since `/jobs/` and `/domain` already match, the test would pass even without `plan/task` or `doing/debug`. But the task description says: \"`task_info_section`/`debug_context` 变量值已变真实路径片段，非 plan_dir 等变量名字面量\". So the intent is that `task_info_section` and `debug_context` variables' VALUES become real path fragments (like `plan/taskN.md`, `doing/debug/`).\n\nBut plan doesn't use task_info_section/debug_context. The dry-run test is `plan --dry-run`, which uses the plan template. So how do `task_info_section`/`debug_context` get into plan output?\n\nHmm wait, let me re-read the boundary test:\n\n\"边界（模板零改动 + 注入路径）：前置条件 = 重构完成；输入 = 无；操作 = `git diff --stat internal/prompt/templates/`（预期无 diff）+ `./bin/rick plan --dry-run | grep -cE 'plan/task|doing/debug|/jobs/|/domain'`（预期 ≥1；`task_info_section`/`debug_context` 变量值已变真实路径片段，非 `plan_dir` 等变量名字面量）；预期 = 模板无 diff 且 task/debug 路径注入命中。\"\n\nSo the operation is plan --dry-run. The expectation is ≥1 matching line. The parenthetical explains that task_info_section/debug_context variable values have become real path fragments. But plan --dry-run doesn't include task_info_section/debug_context.\n\nActually, maybe the intent is: after refactor, the plan template's method/instance split means the dry-run output now includes the instance section that lists `plan/taskN.md` paths and `doing/debug/` paths? That seems odd for plan.\n\nActually wait — maybe the test is just loosely checking that SOME path fragments appear (which already happens via `/jobs/` and `/domain`). The `plan/task|doing/debug` are alternatives in the regex. Since `/jobs/` and `/domain` already match, the test passes. So the test would already pass with current code! Let me verify by checking current plan --dry-run output.\n\nActually, let me just run the current plan dry-run to see.\n\nBut the key requirement is that the refactor doesn't break this. The test also checks no `{{...}}` in output.\n\nLet me check what the current plan dry-run outputs. Let me build current binary and run. But the current binary `bin/rick` is already built (task4). Let me check its dry-run.\n\nActually, let me first understand the full scope and plan the implementation carefully.\n\nLet me also read the remaining prompt tests and cmd tests that might be affected. But actually, the test command is `go test ./internal/builder/... ./internal/prompt/...` — it does NOT include `./internal/cmd/...`. But `go build ./cmd/rick` must succeed, which requires cmd layer to compile (since it imports builder package if I change it). And existing cmd tests aren't run by task5's test script, but `doing_check` might run something? Let me check doing_check.\n\nActually the doing_check command checks tasks.json parse + debug/bug*.md format. Let me check what it does.\n\nActually wait, let me focus. The task test only runs `go test ./internal/builder/... ./internal/prompt/...`. But `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick` builds the whole cmd package. If I change cmd to import builder, the cmd package must compile. Also, other packages like executor (which imports prompt) must still compile.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the full `go build ./cmd/rick` only builds the main package and its dependencies, which includes internal/cmd, internal/prompt, internal/builder, internal/runtime, internal/config, internal/workspace, internal/executor, internal/parser, internal/git, etc.\n\nSo I must keep everything compiling.\n\nNow, let me also read the cmd tests that reference prompt, because even though task5.py doesn't run `go test ./internal/cmd/...`, the broader `doing_check`/final verification might. But the task's test method only requires `go build` + `go test ./internal/builder/... ./internal/prompt/...`. However, I should still keep `go test ./...` green ideally (the spec §4.4 requires go test ./... all green). But task5 only requires the specific packages. Still, to be safe, I should keep everything green.\n\nActually, let me check whether the full test suite currently passes. That might be too slow (task4 mentioned 265s, 6 min). Let me focus on the required scope but keep compile green.\n\nLet me now design the implementation.\n\n### Design\n\n**Package `internal/builder`**:\n\nFiles:\n1. `xxxxbuilder.go` — RuntimeBuilder interface + Method + AgentDef types + doc comment about extension seam.\n2. `pibuilder.go` — PIBuilder type + BuildPlan/BuildDoing/BuildEasy/BuildHumanLoop/BuildCtrl/BuildDream/BuildLearning methods. Also possibly helper sub-builder methods.\n3. Test files (optional but good): `pibuilder_test.go` testing BuildPlan(\"\") error, method/instance split, path injection.\n\n**Relationship to `internal/prompt`**:\nThe pibuilder wraps `internal/prompt` generators. But the key new behavior is:\n- method/instance split: each BuildXxx returns (method, instance, err).\n- path injection: task_info_section → path, debug_context → path.\n\nNow, how do I reconcile \"生成行为（变量替换、prompts/ 目录产物）一致\" with the method/instance split?\n\nThe task says: \"每个 cmd 的 builder 产出两份产物——method（...）→ 走 system prompt... + instance（job 上下文/路径 → 走 user prompt 文件）\".\n\nSo the pibuilder's BuildXxx returns method + instance. The cmd layer then writes:\n- method → system prompt (via `--append-system-prompt`), which the runtime currently supports (task4 added method file handling? Let me check runtime).\n- instance → user prompt file in prompts/ dir.\n\nWait, but KR4 says \"生成行为（变量替换、prompts/ 目录产物）一致\" — the prompts/ dir artifacts stay consistent. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the runtime package to see if it supports `--append-system-prompt` and how method files are handled."}}
