easy/human-loop/ctrl/dream/learning\n    Content string // method-layer text (system prompt)\n}\n\n// AgentDef describes a runtime-specific custom agent built from methods.\ntype AgentDef struct {\n    Name         string\n    Description  string\n    SystemPrompt string\n}\n\n// RuntimeBuilder is the escaping-layer seam for agent runtimes. pi is the only\n// implementation today (PIBuilder); adding a new runtime (e.g. dsh) means\n// implementing this interface and registering a dshBuilder — cli/handler/env\n// stay unchanged.\ntype RuntimeBuilder interface {\n    Name() string\n    BuildAgents(method []Method) ([]AgentDef, error)\n    BuildPrompt(cmd string, params map[string]string) (string, error)\n}\n```\n\n**pibuilder.go**:\n```go\npackage builder\n\nimport (\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"os\"\n    \"path/filepath\"\n    \"sort\"\n    \"strings\"\n\n    \"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/parser\"\n    \"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/prompt\"\n)\n\ntype PIBuilder struct {\n    mgr *prompt.PromptManager\n}\n\nfunc NewPIBuilder() *PIBuilder {\n    return &PIBuilder{mgr: prompt.NewPromptManager()}\n}\n\nfunc (p *PIBuilder) manager() *prompt.PromptManager {\n    if p.mgr == nil { p.mgr = prompt.NewPromptManager() }\n    return p.mgr\n}\n\nfunc (p *PIBuilder) Name() string { return \"pi\" }\n\nfunc (p *PIBuilder) BuildAgents(method []Method) ([]AgentDef, error) {\n    // pi 方法层通过 --append-system-prompt 注入，不注册额外 agent（think/research/exporter 在 task9 落盘）。\n    return []AgentDef{}, nil\n}\n\nfunc (p *PIBuilder) BuildPrompt(cmd string, params map[string]string) (string, error) {\n    switch cmd {\n    case \"plan\":\n        _, instance, err := p.BuildPlan(params[\"requirement\"], params)\n        return instance, err\n    case \"doing\":\n        _, instance, err := p.BuildDoing(params[\"task_id\"], params)\n        return instance, err\n    ... \n    default:\n        return \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"unknown cmd %q\", cmd)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThen Build* methods. Let me write them to produce (method, instance).\n\nThe method string per command. I'll define a helper `methodPrefix` and per-command method strings.\n\nLet me define method content concisely:\n\n```go\nconst rickMethodPrefix = \"你是 rick 的 coding agent。rick = 方法（自然语言描述），你 = 实现。遵循 loops/skills/domain 体系：先读 domain 事实、按 loop 流程执行、按需加载 skills。\"\n\nfunc planMethod() string {\n    return rickMethodPrefix + \"\\n\\n## Plan 方法（9 步 SOP）\\n\" + `1. Domain 加载...`\n}\n```\n\nActually the method content can be a compact summary. Since it's not tested, I'll keep it meaningful but concise. I'll write a shared `commandMethod(name string) string` returning prefix + a short per-command SOP line.\n\nNow the Build* methods. Let me write them to render the instance via prompt low-level.\n\nImportant: For path injection, I need helper functions to compute paths. Let me define in builder:\n- `resolveRickBinPath()` — same as prompt's.\n- `extractJobID()` — same as prompt's.\n\nActually, I can just reimplement these small helpers in builder (they're tiny).\n\nLet me now write each Build*:\n\n#### BuildPlan\n```go\nfunc (p *PIBuilder) BuildPlan(requirement string, params map[string]string) (string, string, error) {\n    if requirement == \"\" {\n        return \"\", \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"requirement cannot be empty\")\n    }\n    tmpl, err := p.manager().LoadTemplate(\"plan\")\n    if err != nil { return \"\", \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"failed to load plan template: %w\", err) }\n\n    rickDir := getParam(params, \"rick_dir\")\n    jobPlanDir := getParam(params, \"job_plan_dir\")\n    if jobPlanDir == \"\" { jobPlanDir = filepath.Join(rickDir, \"jobs\", \"job_N\", \"plan\") }\n\n    loopsDir := filepath.Join(rickDir, \"loops\")\n    domainDir := filepath.Join(rickDir, \"domain\")\n\n    b := prompt.NewPromptBuilder(tmpl)\n    b.SetVariable(\"loops_context\", prompt.LoadLoopsContext(loopsDir))\n    b.SetVariable(\"domain_dir\", domainDir)\n    b.SetVariable(\"user_requirement\", requirement)\n    b.SetVariable(\"job_plan_dir\", jobPlanDir)\n    b.SetVariable(\"rick_bin_path\", resolveRickBinPath())\n    b.SetVariable(\"job_id\", extractJobID(jobPlanDir))\n    // skill paths (dry-run placeholders)\n    b.SetVariable(\"grilling_skill_path\", \"<tmp>/rick-plan-prompts/skill_grilling.md\")\n    b.SetVariable(\"tdd_skill_path\", \"<tmp>/rick-plan-skill-tdd-zh-*.md\")\n    b.SetVariable(\"testing_anti_patterns_path\", \"<tmp>/rick-plan-skill-testing-anti-patterns-zh-*.md\")\n\n    instance, err := b.Build()\n    if err != nil { return \"\", \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"failed to build plan prompt: %w\", err) }\n\n    return planMethod(), instance, nil\n}\n```\n\nThis matches the existing GeneratePlanPrompt behavior (dry-run). Good. But wait, the existing GeneratePlanPrompt sets `job_plan_dir` to the passed value (jobPlanDir), and `rick_bin_path` to resolveRickBinPath, and `job_id` to extractJobID(jobPlanDir). It does NOT set `loops_dir`/`skills_dir` (only loops_context + domain_dir). Good, matches.\n\nThe plan dry-run output should be identical to current (since I mirror GeneratePlanPrompt). \n\nBut wait — the plan --dry-run currently outputs the FULL plan template as one blob (instance). With method/instance split, does the dry-run print both method and instance? The test checks `plan --dry-run | grep ...` and no `{{`. If cmd's runPlanDryRun prints the instance (and maybe method), it's fine as long as instance has paths and no braces.\n\nActually, I realize the plan dry-run should probably print the instance only (or method + instance). For the boundary test, it just greps the combined output. Let me have runPlanDryRun print the instance (the user prompt), and maybe also print the method separately with a header. But to keep output clean and avoid confusing, I'll print just the instance for now (matching current behavior which prints only the prompt content).\n\nWait, but the task says \"method 内容进 system prompt\" — in dry-run, there's no actual system prompt injection (no pi call). So dry-run printing the instance (user prompt) is the meaningful part. I'll print instance only. Actually, to be transparent, I could print a \"method\" section too, but that might confuse the grep? No, grep only counts matching lines; extra method text won't break it (unless method contains `{{`? It won't). Let me keep it simple: print instance only, same as current.\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider — maybe print both method and instance for dry-run to be faithful to the \"两份产物\" concept. But the existing dry-run tests (dryrun_integration_test.go) might assert specific content. Let me check those tests.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_KbUtL2lT5iTeyAtBj5Qd7937","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/internal/cmd/dryrun_integration_test.go"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":3349,"output":2657,"cacheRead":130560,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2589,"totalTokens":136566,"cost":{"input":0.001456815,"output":0.00231159,"cacheRead":0.00047328,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.004241685}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1786903734267,"responseId":"4b4593a9-ee44-4070-98a3-b5d6829ff0ec","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"}}
