{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"These dry-run tests are in cmd package. They reference runPlanDryRun and runLearningDryRun. If I change runPlanDryRun's internals but keep the function signature and behavior (print full prompt), these tests pass.\n\nThe plan dry-run test asserts:\n- output contains \"[DRY-RUN]\"\n- contains \"Plan 阶段\" or \"plan\"\n- ≥5 lines\n- no \"{{job_plan_dir}}\"\n- contains \"loops\"\n\nMy builder.BuildPlan instance contains all these. Good.\n\nNow, since task5's test doesn't run cmd tests, but task6 will, I should keep these passing. So I'll keep runPlanDryRun/runLearningDryRun/runDoingDryRun working.\n\nNow, let me design the full builder API and then rewrite cmd files.\n\nLet me define the builder methods with the exact signatures and semantics. I'll now also decide on the file-saving methods.\n\nTo keep it manageable, I'll make the builder expose:\n\n1. Pure Build* methods (return method, instance):\n   - `BuildPlan(requirement string, params map[string]string) (method string, instance string, err error)`\n   - `BuildDoing(taskID string, params map[string]string) (method string, instance string, err error)`\n   - `BuildEasy(requirement string, params map[string]string) (method string, instance string, err error)`\n   - `BuildHumanLoop(topic string, params map[string]string) (method string, instance string, err error)`\n   - `BuildCtrl(params map[string]string) (method string, instance string, err error)`\n   - `BuildDream(params map[string]string) (method string, instance string, err error)`\n   - `BuildLearning(params map[string]string) (method string, instance string, err error)`\n\nWait, but the test checks `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\")` with nil params. My BuildPlan has `(requirement string, params map[string]string)`. Good.\n\nFor the others, the test only checks method NAMES exist via regex `\\)\\s*BuildDoing\\s*\\(`. So signatures are free.\n\n2. File-saving methods (write instance + skills to prompts/ dir, return paths):\n   - `SavePlanPrompt(requirement string, jobPlanDir string, rickDir string) (promptFile string, method string, err error)`\n   - `SaveDoingPrompt(taskID string, doingDir string, rickDir string) (promptFile string, method string, skillFiles []string, err error)`\n   - `SaveEasyPrompt(jobID string, requirement string, rickDir string, ctxPath string) (promptFile string, method string, skillFiles []string, err error)`\n   - `SaveEasyLearningPrompt(jobID string, rickDir string) (promptFile string, method string, err error)`\n   - `SaveHumanLoopPrompt(topic string, rfcDir string, draftDir string, loopDir string) (mainFile string, thinkFile string, researchFile string, exporterFile string, method string, err error)`\n   - `SaveCtrlPrompt(jobID string, rickDir string) (promptFile string, method string, err error)`\n   - `SaveDreamPrompt(jobIDs []string, rickDir string) (promptFile string, method string, err error)`\n   - `SaveLearningPrompt(data *LearningData, learningDir string, promptsDir string) (promptFile string, method string, err error)`\n\nHmm, this is a LOT. And `SaveLearningPrompt` needs the `ExecutionData`-like type, but that's in cmd package (ExecutionData uses executor.TasksJSON). I'd need a builder-side type.\n\nThis is getting really large. Let me step back and reconsider scope.\n\nGiven the constraints and risk, maybe a better approach:\n\n**Keep the existing `internal/prompt` generators as the \"子 builder\" (底层能力), and have the builder package provide the unified entry + method/instance split + path injection, while cmd delegates file-writing to builder helper methods that internally call prompt's low-level file writers (WriteSkillFile, EnsurePromptsDir, etc.) and render path-injected instances via PromptBuilder.**\n\nActually, the cleanest minimal-risk approach that satisfies the test + KRs:\n\n1. `internal/builder` package with pibuilder (Build* pure methods + Name/BuildAgents/BuildPrompt) + xxxxbuilder (interface/types).\n2. cmd layer: for each command, replace `prompt.Generate*` calls with `builder.NewPIBuilder().Build*` (for dry-run) and for real execution, keep using the EXISTING `prompt.Generate*File` functions BUT re-export them through builder? No...\n\nWait. Let me re-read KR4 once more very carefully:\n\n\"cmd 层 import `internal/prompt` 的调用方改为 import builder 包（executor 仍引用 prompt 底层能力，task8 删除 executor 后自然消失）；生成行为（变量替换、prompts/ 目录产物）一致\"\n\nInterpretation: The callers in cmd that import internal/prompt should change to import the builder package. The generation behavior (variable replacement, prompts/ dir artifacts) should stay consistent.\n\nSo the builder package must expose everything cmd needs. The builder package is allowed to import prompt (it IS the composition layer).\n\nI think the volume is unavoidable if I want full fidelity. But I can reduce by having the builder's Save* methods be the primary real-execution API, and Build* be the dry-run API.\n\nActually, here's a simpler idea to reduce surface: **Have the Build* methods accept a params map that, when it contains `\"save_dir\"` (prompts dir), also write the files; otherwise pure.** But that's implicit.\n\nAlternatively, I realize I can reuse the existing prompt file generators for the Save* path, and ONLY add path injection to the Build* (dry-run) path. But KR3 requires the actual execution to inject paths too.\n\nHmm, wait. Let me reconsider what \"actual execution\" means in task5. In task5, the actual execution still goes through `runtime.CallCLI` (interactive), using a prompt file. The task6 will move to handler + method/instance. So for task5, the \"actual execution\" path (plan/easy/human-loop/ctrl/dream/learning) still writes a single prompt file (instance) and calls CallCLI. The method isn't actually injected via `--append-system-prompt` yet (that's task6/7 wiring; task4 already added the CallCLI extraArgs support but cmd doesn't pass method yet).\n\nSo in task5, the real execution path can still write a single combined prompt file (instance), and the method is computed but perhaps not yet wired into CallCLI (since CallCLI's method injection is a task6 concern).\n\nActually, re-reading task4's KR and task6's note: \"plan/easy 仍走 runtime.CallCLI（交互 TUI，method 经 --append-system-prompt 注入）\" — this is task6's target. And task4 already made `CallCLI` support `--append-system-prompt` via extraArgs. But wiring method into the cmd's CallCLI call is task6.\n\nSo for task5, the cmd layer's real execution can:\n1. Call builder to get (method, instance).\n2. Write instance to prompts/ file (via builder's Save method).\n3. Call `runtime.CallCLI(verbose, cfg, promptFile, ModeInteractive)` — WITHOUT method yet (or WITH method via extraArgs? task6 does it).\n\nActually, to be safe and forward-compatible, I can pass method via extraArgs now: `runtime.CallCLI(verbose, cfg, promptFile, ModeInteractive, \"--append-system-prompt\", methodFile)`. But that requires writing method to a temp file (CallCLI doesn't handle method text, only extraArgs). Hmm, task4 said \"CallCLI 同样经 extraArgs 支持 --append-system-prompt <methodFile> 注入\". So the CALLER writes the method file and passes the path.\n\nThis is getting too deep for task5. Let me keep task5 minimal: cmd writes instance to prompts/ (via builder Save), computes method (via builder), but does NOT wire method into CallCLI yet (leave a TODO for task6/7). This keeps behavior consistent (same as current) and avoids over-reach.\n\nSo the cmd real-execution path:\n```go\npb := builder.NewPIBuilder()\npromptFile, method, err := pb.SavePlanPrompt(requirement, jobPlanDir, rickDir)\n_ = method // task6 经 --append-system-prompt 注入\nruntime.CallCLI(verbose, cfg, promptFile, ModeInteractive)\n```\n\nOK. Now I need Save* methods. Let me define them to reuse prompt's low-level capabilities but with path injection where required.\n\nActually, for plan, there's no task_info_section/debug_context, so SavePlanPrompt can just render the same as BuildPlan but write the file + skills. For doing/easy, Save* must inject paths.\n\nLet me now write the full builder implementation. It's going to be a substantial file. Let me be careful.\n\nLet me define helper functions in builder for path injection:\n\n```go\n// taskPath returns the plan/taskN.md path for a task ID.\nfunc taskPath(planDir, taskID string) string {\n    if taskID == \"\" { taskID = \"taskN\" }\n    return filepath.Join(planDir, taskID+\".md\")\n}\n\n// debugDirPath returns the doing/debug/ directory path.\nfunc debugDirPath(doingDir string) string {\n    return filepath.Join(doingDir, \"debug\")\n}\n```\n\nNow, for doing instance rendering (path injection), I mirror GenerateDoingPrompt but:\n- `task_info_section` = taskPath(planDir, taskID)\n- `debug_context` = debugDirPath(doingDir)\n- `requirement` = \"\" (or keep empty)\n- `grilling_section` = \"\"\n- `import_ctx_content` = \"\"\n- `session_wrap_section` = \"\"\n- `loops_context` = LoadLoopsContext(loopsDir)\n- `skills_context` = LoadSkillsContext(skillsDir)\n- `doing_loop_content` = loadDoingLoopContent(domainDir, debugSkillPath) — but this is unexported in prompt! I need an equivalent in builder.\n- `loop_step_header` = \"## 第一步：执行 Doing Loop\"\n- `check_step_header` = \"\\n---\\n\\n## 第二步：格式检查\"\n- `rick_bin_path`, `check_command`, `job_id`\n\nThe `loadDoingLoopContent` and `stripYAMLFrontmatter` are unexported in prompt. I can reimplement in builder, or export them from prompt. To avoid modifying prompt too much (and risking its tests), I'll reimplement small versions in builder using `prompt.LoadCoreSkills([]string{\"doing_loop\"})`.\n\nActually, `prompt.LoadCoreSkills` is exported. `stripYAMLFrontmatter` is unexported. I'll reimplement `stripYAMLFrontmatter` in builder.\n\nWait, but there's also the easy prompt's `buildGrillingSection`, `buildCtxSection`, `buildSessionWrapSection`, `loadDebugContextLocal`, `buildRequirementSection` — all unexported in prompt. And `GenerateEasyLearningPromptFile` uses `buildEasyTaskResults`.\n\nHmm, this is a lot of reimplementation. \n\nLet me reconsider: maybe it's better to do the path injection INSIDE the prompt package's generators, but guard it so existing tests still pass. How?\n\nThe existing tests use a custom `doingTemplate` (via `NewPromptManager(tmpDir)` with a temp dir containing their own doing.md) for the content-injection tests. They assert content. If I change `GenerateDoingPrompt` to inject paths, those tests break.\n\nBut wait — maybe I can add NEW functions in the prompt package for path injection, and keep the old content-injection functions unchanged. E.g., add `GenerateDoingPromptInstance(taskID, doingDir, rickDir string) (string, error)` that injects paths. Then the builder calls these new functions. This keeps existing tests green and centralizes the logic in prompt (where the template/embed knowledge lives), avoiding reimplementation in builder.\n\nThis seems cleaner! The builder package then becomes a thin composition layer:\n- `BuildDoing` calls `prompt.GenerateDoingPromptInstance(...)` (new path-injection function) for instance, and `prompt.LoadCoreSkills` for method.\n- Save methods call new prompt functions too.\n\nBut wait, KR says \"PromptBuilder/PromptManager/模板 embed 保留为底层能力\" and \"plan_prompt.go/doing_prompt.go/... 生成器 → 子 builder，由新建 pibuilder 统一入口组合\". So the prompt generators ARE the sub-builders. Adding path-injection variants to prompt and having pibuilder compose them is exactly aligned.\n\nSo the plan:\n- In `internal/prompt`, add new exported functions (in new file `instance_prompt.go` or modify existing) that inject paths:\n  - `GeneratePlanInstance(requirement, jobPlanDir, rickDir string) (string, error)` — but plan already injects paths (domain_dir, job_plan_dir); no content→path change needed for plan. Actually plan's only \"content\" is loops_context (frontmatter summary, which is retained). So plan instance = existing GeneratePlanPrompt basically. I can reuse GeneratePlanPrompt for plan instance, OR add an alias.\n\nHmm wait, but the builder's BuildPlan returns (method, instance). The instance for plan = GeneratePlanPrompt output (which is the current dry-run output). So BuildPlan can just call prompt.GeneratePlanPrompt for instance and return method.\n\nActually, that's simplest! For plan, BuildPlan:\n```go\ninstance, err := prompt.GeneratePlanPrompt(requirement, jobPlanDir, rickDir)\n```\nBut GeneratePlanPrompt has signature `(requirement, jobPlanDir, rickDir string)`. I can pass params.\n\nBut wait — does the builder importing prompt and calling GeneratePlanPrompt count as \"组合现有子 builder\"? Yes. And \"PromptBuilder/PromptManager/模板 embed 保留为底层能力\" — yes.\n\nBut there's the KR3 path injection issue for plan: plan doesn't have task_info_section/debug_context, so no change needed. The boundary test for plan --dry-run just checks path fragments, which GeneratePlanPrompt already produces. Good.\n\nFor doing/easy/learning/human-loop/ctrl/dream, I need path-injection variants. Let me add to prompt:\n\n- `GenerateDoingInstance(taskID string, doingDir string, planDir string, rickDir string, jobID string, checkCommand string) (string, error)` — injects paths.\n\nHmm, but the existing `GenerateDoingPrompt` signature is `(task *parser.Task, retryCount int, contextMgr *ContextManager, manager *PromptManager)`. Adding a new function with a different signature is fine.\n\nLet me design the new prompt functions to be minimal and path-injecting:\n\nActually, wait. Let me reconsider whether to even change cmd's doing/easy/etc. to path injection in task5, given the complexity. The task5 test only checks plan --dry-run (not doing/easy). The KR3 is about the builder producing path injection. If I implement BuildDoing/BuildEasy with path injection in builder (or prompt), that satisfies KR3. The cmd doing/easy dry-run would then also show paths (which is fine, and actually desired).\n\nLet me check: does any existing test assert doing/easy dry-run contains task CONTENT (not path)? Let me check cmd tests for doing/easy dry-run.\n\nFrom grep earlier, `tools_test.go` references `runDoingDryRun`. Let me check what it asserts."}}
