{"type":"message_update","assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"`TestBuildLearningPrompt` asserts the prompt contains \"task1\" and \"abc123\" (from tasks.json task results table). `TestBuildLearningPromptInjectsDraftDir` asserts draft_dir resolved and no unreplaced {{draft_dir}}. These tests use `buildLearningPrompt`, which is a cmd-local function.\n\nIf I keep `buildLearningPrompt` in cmd/learning.go as a wrapper that delegates to builder's learning save method (which uses path injection for debug_content?), I need to make sure:\n- \"task1\" and \"abc123\" still appear (from task_execution_results table) — that's already path-based (task results table contains content). Actually `task_execution_results` is a content table built from tasks.json. Is that \"content injection\" that should become path? The KR3 mentions task_info_section/debug_context/OKR/SPEC, NOT task_execution_results. So task_execution_results can stay as content (it's a small table). Keep it.\n- debug_content: currently injected as content (DebugContent). KR3 says \"debug_context\" → path. But learning uses `debug_content` (different variable). Hmm. The KR3 says \"debug_context → doing/debug/ 路径\". learning uses `{{debug_content}}`. Should I change learning's debug_content to path? The task description focuses on task_info_section/debug_context. learning's `debug_content` is a different variable. To be safe, I'll leave learning's debug_content as content (since it's not explicitly named, and the existing tests assert debug content indirectly? Actually TestBuildLearningPrompt asserts \"task1\"/\"abc123\" but not debug content).\n\nActually, `TestBuildLearningPromptInjectsDraftDir` has DebugContent empty, so it goes to \"（本次 job 无 debug.md 记录）\". No content assertion on debug.\n\nHmm, but wait — the learning debug_content in the template is `{{debug_content}}`. The KR3 says debug_context → path. This is about the doing template's `{{debug_context}}`. I'll only change the doing/easy `debug_context` and `task_info_section` variables, not learning's `debug_content`. That's a reasonable scoping.\n\nOK. Given the complexity, here's my final decision on architecture to balance fidelity and risk:\n\n**Approach: Add path-injection generation functions to `internal/prompt` (new file `path_injection.go`), keep existing generators untouched, and create `internal/builder` as a thin composition layer. Then update cmd to use builder.**\n\nWait, but if I add path-injection functions to prompt, that's fine (new file, no changes to existing functions/tests). The builder calls them. This centralizes template knowledge in prompt (where it belongs) and keeps builder thin.\n\nActually, even simpler: I can implement the path-injection rendering directly in the builder package using prompt's exported low-level API (PromptManager.LoadTemplate, PromptBuilder.SetVariable, LoadLoopsContext, LoadSkillsContext, LoadCoreSkills, WriteSkillFile, WriteSkillFileWithVars, EnsurePromptsDir). The only unexported helpers I need to replicate are:\n- `stripYAMLFrontmatter`\n- `loadDoingLoopContent` (uses stripYAMLFrontmatter + LoadCoreSkills)\n- `resolveRickBinPath`\n- `extractJobID`\n\nThese are tiny. I'll replicate them in builder. This avoids touching prompt at all (except maybe exporting nothing), keeping prompt tests 100% safe.\n\nLet me go with implementing in builder directly. This is cleaner for the \"builder三件\" concept (builder contains the composition + path injection; prompt is just the low-level capability).\n\nSo builder will need a fair amount of code. Let me write it carefully.\n\n### Detailed builder implementation\n\nI'll write `internal/builder/pibuilder.go` with:\n\n```go\npackage builder\n\nimport (\n    \"fmt\"\n    \"os\"\n    \"path/filepath\"\n    \"sort\"\n    \"strings\"\n\n    \"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/prompt\"\n    \"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/workspace\"\n)\n\ntype PIBuilder struct {\n    mgr *prompt.PromptManager\n}\n\nfunc NewPIBuilder() *PIBuilder { return &PIBuilder{mgr: prompt.NewPromptManager()} }\n\nfunc (p *PIBuilder) manager() *prompt.PromptManager {\n    if p.mgr == nil { p.mgr = prompt.NewPromptManager() }\n    return p.mgr\n}\n\nfunc (p *PIBuilder) Name() string { return \"pi\" }\n\nfunc (p *PIBuilder) BuildAgents(method []Method) ([]AgentDef, error) {\n    return []AgentDef{}, nil\n}\n\nfunc (p *PIBuilder) BuildPrompt(cmd string, params map[string]string) (string, error) {\n    switch cmd {\n    case \"plan\":\n        _, instance, err := p.BuildPlan(params[\"requirement\"], params)\n        return instance, err\n    case \"doing\":\n        _, instance, err := p.BuildDoing(params[\"task_id\"], params)\n        return instance, err\n    case \"easy\":\n        _, instance, err := p.BuildEasy(params[\"requirement\"], params)\n        return instance, err\n    case \"human-loop\":\n        _, instance, err := p.BuildHumanLoop(params[\"topic\"], params)\n        return instance, err\n    case \"ctrl\":\n        _, instance, err := p.BuildCtrl(params)\n        return instance, err\n    case \"dream\":\n        _, instance, err := p.BuildDream(params)\n        return instance, err\n    case \"learning\":\n        _, instance, err := p.BuildLearning(params)\n        return instance, err\n    default:\n        return \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"unknown cmd %q\", cmd)\n    }\n}\n```\n\nThen method content helpers:\n\n```go\nconst rickMethodPrefix = `你是 rick 的 coding agent：rick 承载「方法」（loops/skills/domain 体系），你负责「实现」。始终先读 domain 事实、按 loop 流程执行、按需加载 skills；任务以可验证的测试方法为成功标准。`\n\nfunc methodFor(cmd string) string {\n    var sop string\n    switch cmd {\n    case \"plan\":\n        sop = planMethodSOP()\n    case \"doing\":\n        sop = doingMethodSOP()\n    ...\n    }\n    return rickMethodPrefix + \"\\n\\n\" + sop\n}\n```\n\nActually, I'll just make a `commandMethod(cmd string) string` returning prefix + short per-command SOP text. Keep it concise.\n\nNow the Build* methods. Let me writ