t plan dry-run outputs. Let me build current binary and run. But the current binary `bin/rick` is already built (task4). Let me check its dry-run.\n\nActually, let me first understand the full scope and plan the implementation carefully.\n\nLet me also read the remaining prompt tests and cmd tests that might be affected. But actually, the test command is `go test ./internal/builder/... ./internal/prompt/...` — it does NOT include `./internal/cmd/...`. But `go build ./cmd/rick` must succeed, which requires cmd layer to compile (since it imports builder package if I change it). And existing cmd tests aren't run by task5's test script, but `doing_check` might run something? Let me check doing_check.\n\nActually the doing_check command checks tasks.json parse + debug/bug*.md format. Let me check what it does.\n\nActually wait, let me focus. The task test only runs `go test ./internal/builder/... ./internal/prompt/...`. But `go build -o bin/rick ./cmd/rick` builds the whole cmd package. If I change cmd to import builder, the cmd package must compile. Also, other packages like executor (which imports prompt) must still compile.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the full `go build ./cmd/rick` only builds the main package and its dependencies, which includes internal/cmd, internal/prompt, internal/builder, internal/runtime, internal/config, internal/workspace, internal/executor, internal/parser, internal/git, etc.\n\nSo I must keep everything compiling.\n\nNow, let me also read the cmd tests that reference prompt, because even though task5.py doesn't run `go test ./internal/cmd/...`, the broader `doing_check`/final verification might. But the task's test method only requires `go build` + `go test ./internal/builder/... ./internal/prompt/...`. However, I should still keep `go test ./...` green ideally (the spec §4.4 requires go test ./... all green). But task5 only requires the specific packages. Still, to be safe, I should keep everything green.\n\nActually, let me check whether the full test suite currently passes. That might be too slow (task4 mentioned 265s, 6 min). Let me focus on the required scope but keep compile green.\n\nLet me now design the implementation.\n\n### Design\n\n**Package `internal/builder`**:\n\nFiles:\n1. `xxxxbuilder.go` — RuntimeBuilder interface + Method + AgentDef types + doc comment about extension seam.\n2. `pibuilder.go` — PIBuilder type + BuildPlan/BuildDoing/BuildEasy/BuildHumanLoop/BuildCtrl/BuildDream/BuildLearning methods. Also possibly helper sub-builder methods.\n3. Test files (optional but good): `pibuilder_test.go` testing BuildPlan(\"\") error, method/instance split, path injection.\n\n**Relationship to `internal/prompt`**:\nThe pibuilder wraps `internal/prompt` generators. But the key new behavior is:\n- method/instance split: each BuildXxx returns (method, instance, err).\n- path injection: task_info_section → path, debug_context → path.\n\nNow, how do I reconcile \"生成行为（变量替换、prompts/ 目录产物）一致\" with the method/instance split?\n\nThe task says: \"每个 cmd 的 builder 产出两份产物——method（...）→ 走 system prompt... + instance（job 上下文/路径 → 走 user prompt 文件）\".\n\nSo the pibuilder's BuildXxx returns method + instance. The cmd layer then writes:\n- method → system prompt (via `--append-system-prompt`), which the runtime currently supports (task4 added method file handling? Let me check runtime).\n- instance → user prompt file in prompts/ dir.\n\nWait, but KR4 says \"生成行为（变量替换、prompts/ 目录产物）一致\" — the prompts/ dir artifacts stay consistent. Hmm.\n\nLet me look at the runtime package to see if it supports `--append-system-prompt` and how method files are handled.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_eIYjGgK9xSz1BeTuHBg96795","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/internal/runtime/cli.go"}},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_01_LsLvG1wPDhAxWuGzjs9c5679","name":"read","arguments":{"path":"/workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/internal/runtime/runtime.go"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":4023,"output":2612,"cacheRead":98816,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":2500,"totalTokens":105451,"cost":{"input":0.001750005,"output":0.00227244,"cacheRead":0.000358208,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.004380653}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1786903468341,"responseId":"650d65b9-26c4-4415-a00c-0c733d091ad2","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_00_eIYjGgK9xSz1BeTuHBg96795","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"package runtime\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"strings\"\n\n\t\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/config\"\n)\n\n// CLIMode selects how the pi binary is invoked.\ntype CLIMode int\n\nconst (\n\t// ModeInteractive runs `pi [extraArgs] <promptFile>` with stdin/stdout/stderr\n\t// inherited from rick (human-facing interactive session).\n\tModeInteractive CLIMode = iota\n\t// ModePrint runs `pi -p [extraArgs] <promptFile>` in non-interactive print\n\t// mode, forwarding stdout/stderr to the terminal. pi has no permission popups,\n\t// so no --dangerously-skip-permissions flag is needed.\n\tModePrint\n)\n\n// FindBinary resolves the pi binary path: cfg.PiPath if set, otherwise rick's\n// self-contained runtime (~/.rick/pi/agent/runtime) if installed, otherwise\n// \"pi\" looked up in PATH. cfg may be nil (PATH-only lookup, used by auto-fix\n// sites that have no config context). Returns an error if pi is neither\n// configured, installed in the managed runtime, nor on PATH.\nfunc FindBinary(cfg *config.Config) (string, error) {\n\tif cfg != nil && cfg.PiPath != \"\" {\n\t\treturn cfg.PiPath, nil\n\t}\n\tif bin := RuntimeBin(); FileExists(bin) {\n\t\treturn bin, nil\n\t}\n\tpath, err := exec.LookPath(\"pi\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", fmt.Errorf(\"pi binary not found (set pi_path in config, run rick tools init-pi, or install pi): %w\", err)\n\t}\n\treturn path, nil\n}\n\n// piPathOrDefault returns cfg.PiPath, else rick's managed runtime pi if\n// installed, else \"pi\" without a PATH check. Used by CallCLI where a missing\n// binary should surface as a natural exec error rather than a pre-flight\n// failure.\nfunc piPathOrDefault(cfg *config.Config) string {\n\tif cfg != nil && cfg.PiPath != \"\" {\n\t\treturn cfg.PiPath\n\t}\n\tif bin := RuntimeBin(); FileExists(bin) {\n\t\treturn bin\n\t}\n\treturn \"pi\"\n}\n\n// buildArgs assembles the pi argument list for a given mode. It is split out\n// from CallCLI so flag logic can be unit-tested without shelling out.\n//\n// extraArgs are pi flags passed through verbatim (e.g. \"--session\", \"<id>\" or\n// \"--continue\"). promptFile is appended last and omitted when empty (used by\n// easy.go's resume path, which passes only flags).\nfunc buildArgs(mode CLIMode, promptFile string, extraArgs ...string) []string {\n\targs := make([]string, 0, len(extraArgs)+2)\n\tif mode == ModePrint {\n\t\targs = append(args, \"-p\")\n\t}\n\targs = append(args, extraArgs...)\n\tif promptFile != \"\" {\n\t\targs = append(args, promptFile)\n\t}\n\treturn args\n}\n\n// CallCLI invokes the pi binary for the direct (non-AgentSession) call sites.\n// Interactive inherits stdio; Print forwards stdout/stderr to the terminal.\n// verbose mirrors the previous callClaudeCodeCLI [INFO] log line.\n//\n// pi flags come from two sources, in this order: cfg.PiExtraArgs (global, e.g.\n// --provider/--model/--api-key) then extraArgs (per-call, e.g. --session <id>).\n// promptFile is always appended last.\nfunc CallCLI(verbose bool, cfg *config.Config, promptFile string, mode CLIMode, extraArgs ...string) error {\n\tpiBin := piPathOrDefault(cfg)\n\tmerged := mergeExtraArgs(cfg, extraArgs)\n\targs := buildArgs(mode, promptFile, merged...)\n\n\tif verbose {\n\t\tfmt.Printf(\"[INFO] Executing: %s %s\\n\", piBin, strings.Join(args, \" \"))\n\t}\n\n\tcmd := exec.Command(piBin, args...)\n\tif mode == ModeInteractive {\n\t\tcmd.Stdin = os.Stdin\n\t}\n\tcmd.Stdout = os.Stdout\n\tcmd.Stderr = os.Stderr\n\t// rick manages pi's config under ~/.rick/pi/agent (PI_CODING_AGENT_DIR),\n\t// isolated from the user's own ~/.pi — every pi subprocess must see it.\n\tcmd.Env = AgentEnv()\n\n\tif err := cmd.Run(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn fmt.Errorf(\"pi CLI failed: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\treturn nil\n}\n\n// mergeExtraArgs prepends cfg.PiExtraArgs to the caller's extraArgs (global\n// config flags first, then per-call flags). cfg may be nil.\nfunc mergeExtraArgs(cfg *config.Config, extraArgs []string) []string {\n\tif cfg == nil || len(cfg.PiExtraArgs) == 0 {\n\t\treturn extraArgs\n\t}\n\tmerged := make([]string, 0, len(cfg.PiExtraArgs)+len(extraArgs))\n\tmerged = append(merged, cfg.PiExtraArgs...)\n\tmerged = append(merged, extraArgs...)\n\treturn merged\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1786903508197},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_01_LsLvG1wPDhAxWuGzjs9c5679","toolName":"read","content":[{"type":"text","text":"package runtime\n\nimport (\n\t\"fmt\"\n\t\"os\"\n\t\"os/exec\"\n\t\"time\"\n\n\t\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/agent\"\n\t\"github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/config\"\n)\n\n// Trace is the behavioral trace of a single runtime run. It carries the same\n// information the old act-path + session pair held: the session identity, the\n// tool-call history, the agent's final message, the raw JSONL log path, the\n// self-timed duration, and whether the runtime reported a clean settle.\ntype Trace struct {\n\tSessionID    string\n\tToolCalls    []agent.ToolCall\n\tFinalMessage string\n\tRawLogPath   string\n\tDuration     time.Duration\n\tSettled      bool\n}\n\n// Runtime is the seam for agent runtimes (pi today, dsh later). Handlers depend\n// on this interface rather than a concrete piRuntime, so adding a new runtime\n// means implementing and registering dshRuntime without touching the handlers\n// or the method-layer templates.\n//\n// methodText is the method-layer system prompt (rick 方法描述): it is injected\n// before the session via `--append-system-prompt <methodFile>` so pi keeps its\n// default skeleton. promptFile is the instance context (user prompt).\ntype Runtime interface {\n\tName() string\n\tRun(methodText string, promptFile string, cfg *config.Config) (sessionID string, trace *Trace, err error)\n}\n\n// piRuntime implements Runtime for the pi coding agent.\ntype piRuntime struct {\n\tpiPath    string\n\textraArgs []string\n}\n\n// NewPiRuntime constructs the pi runtime implementation. piPath may be empty\n// (resolve via config / managed runtime / PATH); extraArgs are passed through to\n// pi (e.g. --provider/--model/--api-key).\nfunc NewPiRuntime(piPath string, extraArgs ...string) *piRuntime {\n\treturn &piRuntime{piPath: piPath, extraArgs: extraArgs}\n}\n\n// Name returns the runtime's identifier (\"pi\").\nfunc (r *piRuntime) Name() string { return \"pi\" }\n\n// Run launches pi for one prompt and returns the parsed session id + trace.\n//\n// methodText (the method layer) is written to a temp file and injected through\n// `--append-system-prompt <methodFile>` — a session-preparation injection that\n// preserves pi's default skeleton and avoids passing long text inline. The temp\n// file is created by the runtime and removed on return (defer cleanup, deleted\n// once used). promptFile is passed last as the user prompt (instance context).\n//\n// This is the runtime contract's skeleton: it is not yet wired into handlers\n// (task8 performs the Execute→Run switch). The AgentExecutor-compatible Execute\n// below remains the wired entry point for the structured doing path.\nfunc (r *piRuntime) Run(methodText string, promptFile string, cfg *config.Config) (string, *Trace, error) {\n\tpiBin := r.piPath\n\tif piBin == \"\" {\n\t\tpiBin = piPathOrDefault(cfg)\n\t}\n\n\t// Method layer → temp file → --append-system-prompt <methodFile>. The runtime\n\t// owns the temp file and deletes it once pi has consumed it (defer).\n\tvar methodFile string\n\tif methodText != \"\" {\n\t\tf, err := os.CreateTemp(\"\", \"rick-method-*.md\")\n\t\tif err != nil {\n\t\t\treturn \"\", nil, fmt.Errorf(\"create method temp file: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tmethodFile = f.Name()\n\t\tif _, err := f.WriteString(methodText); err != nil {\n\t\t\tf.Close()\n\t\t\tos.Remove(methodFile)\n\t\t\treturn \"\", nil, fmt.Errorf(\"write method temp file: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := f.Close(); err != nil {\n\t\t\tos.Remove(methodFile)\n\t\t\treturn \"\", nil, fmt.Errorf(\"close method temp file: %w\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tdefer os.Remove(methodFile)\n\t}\n\n\t// Raw JSONL log: parseStream persists the event stream for the trace. The\n\t// skeleton keeps it in a temp file (task8 routes it to the job dir).\n\trawLog, err := os.CreateTemp(\"\", \"rick-raw-*.log\")\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", nil, fmt.Errorf(\"create raw log temp file: %w\", err)\n\t}\n\trawLogPath := rawLog.Name()\n\trawLog.Close()\n\tdefer os.Remove(rawLogPath)\n\n\tmerged := mergeExtraArgs(cfg, r.extraArgs)\n\targs := make([]string, 0, len(merged)+6)\n\targs = append(args, \"--mode\", \"json\")\n\targs = append(args, merged...)\n\tif methodFile != \"\" {\n\t\targs = append(args, \"--append-system-prompt\", methodFile)\n\t}\n\tif promptFile != \"\" {\n\t\targs = append(args, promptFile)\n\t}\n\n\tcmd := exec.Command(piBin, args...)\n\t// rick-managed pi config isolation (same as CallCLI / Execute).\n\tcmd.Env = AgentEnv()\n\tstdout, err := cmd.StdoutPipe()\n\tif err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", nil, err\n\t}\n\tif err := cmd.Start(); err != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", nil, err\n\t}\n\n\tsess, parseErr := parseStream(stdout, rawLogPath)\n\tcmd.Wait() //nolint\n\tif parseErr != nil {\n\t\treturn \"\", nil, parseErr\n\t}\n\n\ttrace := &Trace{\n\t\tSessionID:    sess.sessionID,\n\t\tToolCalls:    sess.toolCalls,\n\t\tFinalMessage: sess.finalMessage,\n\t\tRawLogPath:   sess.rawLogPath,\n\t\tDuration:     sess.duration,\n\t\tSettled:      sess.settled,\n\t}\n\treturn sess.sessionID, trace, nil\n}\n"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1786903508197},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The runtime already has the method/instance seam in place (from task4). The runtime.Run takes methodText + promptFile. So the builder's method/instance split aligns.\n\nNow, the cmd layer currently calls `runtime.CallCLI(verbose, cfg, promptFile, runtime.ModeInteractive)` for plan and easy/doing via executor. It doesn't yet use `runtime.Run` with method/instance. The task8 will wire handler. But task5 is only about the builder.\n\nWait, but task5 KR4 says \"cmd 层 import internal/prompt 的调用方改为 import builder 包\". So the cmd layer should now call builder package functions instead of prompt package functions. But the cmd layer still uses `runtime.CallCLI` (not `runtime.Run`), which takes a single promptFile.\n\nHmm. This is getting complicated. Let me carefully think about the minimal-but-correct implementation that satisfies the test script and the Key Results, without over-engineering.\n\nKey insight: The test script is the actual gate. Let me enumerate exactly what's needed to pass:\n\n1. `go build -o <tmp>/rick ./cmd/rick` succeeds.\n2. `go test -timeout 180s -v ./internal/builder/... ./internal/prompt/...` succeeds (exit 0).\n3. `internal/prompt/templates/` unchanged (git diff empty).\n4. `<bin> plan --dry-run` exit 0, output contains ≥1 line matching `plan/task|doing/debug|/jobs/|/domain`, and no `{{` in output.\n5. `PIBuilder.BuildPlan(\"\", nil)` returns error containing \"requirement cannot be empty\" (via temp Go program importing `github.com/sunquan/rick/internal/builder`).\n6. Structural: internal/builder exists, xxxxbuilder.go with RuntimeBuilder interface + Method/AgentDef, PIBuilder type + 7 methods, BuildPlan signature.\n\nSo the crucial runtime-visible behavior is only `plan --dry-run`. The cmd layer must produce plan dry-run output that includes path fragments and no unreplaced vars.\n\nNow, does the current code already pass point 4? Let me check by running the current binary.\n\nLet me build and run plan --dry-run with current code to see.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_TffaDsZ36LHLTCmB70CZ0550","name":"bash","arguments":{"command":"cd /workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick && go build -o /tmp/rick_cur ./cmd/rick 2>&1 | head -20 && /tmp/rick_cur plan --dry-run 2>&1 | head -60"}}],"api":"openai-completions","provider":"deepseek","model":"deepseek-v4-pro","usage":{"input":2536,"output":584,"cacheRead":105344,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":484,"totalTokens":108464,"cost":{"input":0.00110316,"output":0.0005080800000000001,"cacheRead":0.000381872,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.001993112}},"stopReason":"toolUse","timestamp":1786903508198,"responseId":"8d6506b4-d306-4b5d-8d8b-8320f8b5b806","rawStopReason":"tool_calls"},{"role":"toolResult","toolCallId":"call_00_TffaDsZ36LHLTCmB70CZ0550","toolName":"bash","content":[{"type":"text","text":"2026/08/17 02:05:25 warn: LoadLoopsContext: no trigger in README.md, skipping\n[DRY-RUN] Plan prompt:\n\n# Rick Plan 阶段\n\n你的任务：**将用户需求转化为可落地执行的任务列表**。\n\n---\n\n## 一、项目上下文\n\n### 项目 Domain（事实性知识库）\n\n路径：`/workdir/sunquan20/AI_CODING/rick/.rick/domain`\n\n> Domain 存储项目的客观事实：环境配置、已知问题与解法、接口规范、构建命令等。规划前必须优先读取。\n\n### 项目 Loops（已有工作流模式）\n\n## 可用的项目 Loops\n\n- **agent-runtime-bootstrap-loop**：\"当需要初始化/迁移/重装 rick 的 agent runtime（pi）及其扩展时触发（如 rick tools init-pi、版本升级、runtime 迁移）\"\n- **do-check-mark-success-loop**：\"当 doing_check 报错 task status != success 或 commit_hash 缺失时触发\"\n- **protocol-redesign-loop**：\"当需要重构 AI agent 的多阶段协议(如人机协作流程、任务执行流程),涉及阶段合并/拆分/反向回流/批判层重设计时触发\"\n- **readme-wiki-sync-loop**：\"当需要编写或重构 README.md、wiki/（用户面向文档）等描述性资料时触发\"\n- **tdd-red-green-refactor-loop**：\"当测试已存在且处于 FAIL 状态，需要通过迭代实现让其变绿时触发（前提：先写测试、当前测试 FAIL、目标是收敛到绿）\"\n\n\n---\n\n## 二、项目探索\n\n如果需要理解当前代码状态，请自行探索项目。使用 Read / Grep / Glob / Bash 等工具，理解：\n- 项目目标（从 README、代码注释、测试用例推断）\n- 技术栈与架构（从依赖文件、目录结构推断）\n- 当前状态与待解决的问题\n\n探索完成后，向用户确认你的理解，再开始任务规划。\n\n---\n\n## 三、任务分解原则\n\n1. **模块化**：每个 task 是独立功能单元，可独立开发和验证\