or task5, the required test is only builder + prompt packages. But I should avoid breaking cmd tests if feasible, because task6 and the final spec require `go test ./...` green. However, task6 explicitly says \"随函数迁移同步改写 internal/cmd/{plan,doing,easy}_test.go 中引用已迁函数的断言\" — meaning task6 will update cmd tests. So it's acceptable that cmd tests reference prompt functions for now? No wait, task6 changes cmd to import handler, and updates tests.\n\nActually, let me re-read KR4 more carefully: \"cmd 层 import internal/prompt 的调用方改为 import builder 包（executor 仍引用 prompt 底层能力...）\". So in task5, cmd should import builder instead of prompt.\n\nThis means cmd test files that import prompt (learning_test.go) would need updating too, OR they'd break `go test ./internal/cmd/...`. But task5's test doesn't run cmd tests. However, to be safe and correct, I should update cmd tests that reference prompt to reference builder (or keep them but they'd fail to compile under `go test ./internal/cmd/...`).\n\nActually, `go test ./internal/cmd/...` would fail to COMPILE if learning_test.go imports prompt AND prompt is still importable (it is). Wait — if cmd package source files no longer import prompt, but a test file in the same package imports prompt, that's fine for compilation (test files are in the same package, can import anything). The issue is only if a test file references an unexported function that was removed, or imports a package that no longer exists.\n\nSince prompt package still exists, `internal/cmd/learning_test.go` importing prompt is fine for compilation. But `buildLearningPrompt` is an unexported function in cmd/learning.go. If I move `buildLearningPrompt` to builder, the test file `learning_test.go` references `buildLearningPrompt` (unexported) — it would fail to compile because the function no longer exists in package cmd.\n\nSo I have two choices:\nA) Keep `buildLearningPrompt` in cmd/learning.go (as a thin wrapper calling builder), so learning_test.go still compiles