err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t\t// Write meaningful SPEC.md so the project-level check passes\n\t\tspecPath := filepath.Join(dir, \".rick\", \"SPEC.md\")\n\t\tspecContent := \"# SPEC\\n## 架构设计\\n本项目采用 Go 标准库实现，无外部依赖。\\n## 编译与运行方法\\n```\\ngo build ./...\\n```\\n\"\n\t\tif err := os.WriteFile(specPath, []byte(specContent), 0644); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Fatal(err)\n\t\t}\n\t\tif err := runPlanCheck(planDir); err != nil {\n\t\t\tt.Errorf(\"expected no error, got: %v\", err)\n\t\t}\n\t})\n\n[340 more lines in file. Use offset=480 to continue.]"}],"isError":false,"timestamp":1786903838993},{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The doing dry-run tests only check \"no error\", not content. So path injection in doing dry-run won't break these cmd tests. Good.\n\nNow, the key remaining concern: I want `go test ./internal/cmd/...` to still compile (for task6 and spec §4.4). Currently learning_test.go imports prompt. If cmd/learning.go no longer imports prompt, learning_test.go still imports prompt (separate file, same package). That's fine for compilation as long as the referenced functions exist:\n- `buildLearningPrompt` (cmd function) — must still exist in cmd package.\n- `prompt.NewPromptManager` — still exists.\n\nSo if I keep `buildLearningPrompt` in cmd/learning.go (even as a wrapper), learning_test.go compiles. And cmd/learning.go would import builder (and maybe not prompt). But learning_test.go imports prompt — that's fine, test file can import prompt independently.\n\nWait, actually there's a subtle issue: if cmd/learning.go's `buildLearningPrompt` is changed to delegate to builder, and learning_test.go still calls `buildLearningPrompt` and expects certain behavior (writes files, etc.), it should still pass as long as behavior is consistent.\n\nLet me read learning_test.go to understand what it asserts.","thinkingSignature":"reasoning_content"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_00_gLvY2cJEitw4qxTer2a71150","name":"read","arguments":{"pa